WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.350
Even in the midst of this remake and reboot-friendly
era, a lot of horror movies are just waiting

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.160
to be remade — and they could be genuinely
spectacular.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.200
Keep your mind open and your grumblings to
a minimum, because here's a look at some horror

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.000
movies that legitimately deserve a remake.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.380
Ever since its release in 1997, this Paul
W.S. Anderson space chiller has divided audiences

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.800
over whether it's a good time or one hell
of a terrible movie.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.700
One thing everyone can agree on is that the
film, in which a space crew investigates an

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:32.110
abandoned ship only to discover it may be
a gateway to hell, is a product of its time

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.940
which hasn't exactly aged well.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.040
In its defense, it also suffered a troubled
production history that heavily impacted the

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:38.620
final cut.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.500
As fans of the film will tell you, this visceral
sci-fi-horror outing was given a preposterously

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.920
tight post-production deadline on top of studio
demands for excessive edits.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.040
Apparently, this deeply affected the pacing
and visual effects of the theatrical cut,

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.580
and it also meant some of the film's scariest
and goriest set pieces were scrapped.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.310
The film's notorious lost footage was unwisely
packaged off to a Transylvanian salt mine

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:03.000
and the resulting damage was apparently irrevocable.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:08.280
In 2019, it was reported that Adam Wingard
is developing a TV adaptation of Event Horizon,

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.650
which could be great, as long as it delivers
all the vital unseen moments and plot development

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.560
that were sadly scrapped from the original.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.960
Event Horizon deserves a remake that can finally
fulfill the gnarly, nasty vision that neither

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.440
Anderson nor the movie's audience ever got
to see.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.580
"What happened to your eyes?"

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.500
"Where we're going, we won't need eyes to
see."

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.960
This stylish 1983 horror movie from director
Tony Scott has it all — and still makes

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.300
for a great, if quaint, viewing experience.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:40.320
The Hunger uses '80s goth and New Wave culture
as its backdrop to explore a story that revolves

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.560
around addiction, sexual politics, and the
anguish of aging.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.170
The Hunger follows a vampire named Miriam
who takes lovers that she turns into vampires.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.950
But there's a catch: While she lives forever,
never aging, her vampiric lovers grow old

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.420
and frail while forced to subsist on human
blood.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.960
In 2009, Warner Bros. were reportedly working
on a remake of The Hunger, though clearly

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.000
that idea seems to have been abandoned fairly
early in development.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.620
While fans of the original underground hit
revealed they weren't happy at the prospect

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.720
of a remake, it certainly deserves one.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.200
It's worth noting that the original ending
of The Hunger was changed at the studio's

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.510
request to leave the film open for sequels
— something cast member Susan Sarandon has

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:17.540
said she regrets.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.340
On the DVD commentary, she said:

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.820
"The powers that be rewrote the ending and
decided that I wouldn't die.

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:25.920
Nobody knew what was going on, and I thought
that was a shame."

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.640
The ambiguous ending of The Hunger is certainly
something that could be fixed with a remake.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.180
A 3D remake of the 1987 cult classic The Gate
was in pre-production in 2009, but sadly died

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:36.920
in development.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.700
Director Tibor Takács, who helmed the original,
told Birth.Movies.Death in 2012:

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.100
"We were on board and involved in the remake
for a while and then we had a difference of

00:02:45.109 --> 00:02:46.620
opinion and we parted ways.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.439
I don’t even know if they shot it or not!

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.880
I can’t get a straight story."

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.260
Alex Winter of Bill &amp; Ted fame was also on
board to direct the remake, which he planned

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:59.239
to use as a launchpad for an entire PG-13
horror franchise, but it unfortunately never

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.220
got off the ground.

00:03:00.329 --> 00:03:04.220
A more modern remake of The Gate could be
perfect for reflecting youthful fears of being

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.880
powerless in the face of a world that seems
out of control.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:14.740
“The old gods, the rightful masters, are
jealous, watching mankind with a hatred as

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.460
boundless as the stars."

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.200
An American remake of Battle Royale has been
at various levels of development since the

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.349
original Japanese release became a cult hit,
though various obstacles have prevented it

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:26.400
from coming to fruition.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.900
Telling the story of a class of schoolchildren
in a totalitarian society who are forced to

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:35.849
fight to the death until a lone survivor emerges,
the film's themes remain painfully relevant

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:37.040
in modern America.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.189
Sadly, that relevance has hit a little too
close to home, with one attempt at a remake

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:43.340
paused following the Virginia Tech Massacre.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:47.339
That same remake was officially dropped in
2012 out of fears that it would look like

00:03:47.339 --> 00:03:51.180
a rip-off of The Hunger Games, even though
Battle Royale was written years before the

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.400
first Hunger Games book.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.680
Still, the potential to develop Battle Royale
for the next generation still exists.

00:03:56.689 --> 00:04:00.591
Given the current sensitivity over mass violence,
especially when it concerns children, it's

00:04:00.591 --> 00:04:05.059
likely to be met with controversy, but maybe
that's exactly the reason why this film deserves

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.060
to be remade.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:10.560
Centered around a pack of practical-joke loving
college students who end up being picked off

00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:14.659
by a mysterious killer with a cruel sense
of humor, April Fool's Day is quite possibly

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.920
one of the dumbest, and most fun, slasher
movies of its time.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.460
Leading the ill-fated group of teens is an
unconventional Final Girl named Muffy St.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.420
John, an eccentric, wealthy teenager with
plenty of mansion space to share with her

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.400
ill-fated coeds.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:32.160
April Fool's Day is packed full of ridiculous
plot twists and red herrings, as well as obnoxious

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.460
rich kids getting slaughtered in ridiculous
ways.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.060
In short, it's everything you could want from
the genre.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:41.300
This film actually did receive a remake in
2008, but the direct-to-video film wasn't

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.740
able to tap into the core comedic appeal of
the original.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.170
With the rise of internet pranksters turned
viral stars like YouTube celebrities, we're

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.860
in what can only be described as a prankster
renaissance.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:54.690
A newly modern remake may not retain the same
cheesy charm as the original, but it could

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:59.180
still be timely and huge amounts of fun — especially
if it means seeing YouTube pranksters getting

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.200
their hilariously hideous comeuppance.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:06.600
The horror genre is long overdue for the arrival
of a smart, grown-up slasher franchise.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:10.320
While most horror fans would likely prefer
an original take on what that would look like,

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.100
there's something intriguing about the prospect
of remaking horror satire The Slumber Party

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.860
Massacre for modern audiences.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:20.340
The subversive cult classic from director
Amy Holden Jones and writer Rita Mae Brown

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.520
was dismissed as little more than a derivative
slasher-by-numbers when it was released

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:25.520
in 1982.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.340
"Dog's in the trash again."

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.080
“So go take care of it."

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:34.040
"What do you think I am, stupid? I'm not going out there alone in the dark."

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:38.540
Since then, the film has since been hailed
for skewering the overt misogyny of the slasher

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.820
genre in its revolt against genre tropes.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.820
Jones has expressed some regrets about having
to include gratuitous nudity in the film's

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.700
early shower scene, which she has said was
unavoidable.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:52.180
Producer Roger Corman insisted on the scene's
inclusion because, according to Jones:

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.220
"Nudity was more important to [Roger Corman]
than sex and you know he has to sell the thing"

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.380
It's a horror trope that's been gradually
forced out of the genre, and even subverted

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:02.230
in modern entries like Midsommar.

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:02.720
Shout!

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.880
Factory acquired Corman's library in 2018
and have reportedly expressed interest in

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:07.940
a potential remake.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:11.820
There are currently plenty of incredible female
horror directors who could take a confident

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.920
swing at it, so here's hoping that they get
the chance to make their bloody mark.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.440
The Return of the Living Dead continues to
be one of the most fun horror movies ever

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:23.610
made, and one which provides a neat time capsule
in celebrating some of the subversive youth

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:24.760
culture of the '80s.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:29.080
The Return of the Living Dead is pure punk
rock, right down to the set dressing and outfits

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:30.100
worn by the cast.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.750
That punk spirit includes authentic depictions
of punk culture, but it also has an aggressively

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:38.800
irreverent anti-establishment stance, which
is emphatically anti-military.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.980
It's rare to find similarly authentic depictions
of young, rebellious subcultures in modern

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:45.840
horror, or anywhere in contemporary cinema,
for that matter.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.231
The fact that there's no modern-day remake
of The Return of the Living Dead could be

00:06:49.231 --> 00:06:53.930
considered a real wasted opportunity when
youth subcultures are arguably the most visible

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:56.240
and politically active they have been in decades.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:00.460
There's definitely an argument to be made
the last decade has given us enough zombie

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:03.340
films and TV shows to last a lifetime and
beyond.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.890
Still, there's still something intriguing
about discovering a fresh zombie narrative

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.210
which has a slightly different story to tell.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.800
A modern-day remake of Sugar Hill could be
the one that could brings fresh life to the

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:13.880
overdone genre.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:18.760
As much a revenge movie as it is a zombie
flick, this underrated 1974 blaxploitation

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:22.740
horror film follows a woman who summons an
army of zombies to seek revenge against the

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.320
mobsters who killed her boyfriend.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.900
With an empowered African-American woman leading
the story, Sugar Hill takes on feminist issues

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:32.240
alongside some sharp commentary regarding
racism and class.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:36.100
Most importantly, it does all of this while
still being extremely entertaining.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:40.150
With the current renaissance of black horror
pushing African-American narratives and concerns

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:44.270
to the forefront of a genre that hasn't always
been so historically inclusive, Sugar Hill

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:47.820
could make for an incredible mainstream Hollywood
remake that's well deserved.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:52.440
Anyone who holds a place for this '80s classic
in their hearts will be well aware of the

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.930
fact that The Monster Squad, in which a group
of witty, talkative kids take on some classic

00:07:56.930 --> 00:08:01.419
monsters in their hometown, is arguably one
of the biggest influences on the current pop

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:04.020
culture trend of kids-vs.-monsters content.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:08.460
From Stranger Things to the Child's Play reboot
and Scary Stories to Tell in the Dark and

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:13.000
the new It, you can't go far without bumping
into stories about children fighting monsters.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.350
In recent years, Monster Squad filmmakers
Fred Dekker and Shane Black even had to let

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:21.100
go of their idea for a 30 years later sequel
to the original because everyone's already

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:22.670
doing exactly that.

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:23.440
As Dekker put it:

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:26.740
"I thought, well, let's make this movie for
the people who fell in love when they were

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.290
kids and say, 'Where is the squad as adults?'

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:31.010
And Shane said to me, 'Well that's it.'

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:32.699
I said, 'Yeah, it's a great idea.'

00:08:32.699 --> 00:08:36.229
And he goes, “No, that's Stephen King's
‘It.’ the first part of the book and the

00:08:36.229 --> 00:08:40.380
first movie is them as kids fighting monsters
and the second movie is them as adults fighting

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:40.880
monsters.”

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:44.520
But it's the ubiquity of these tropes which
could actually make for a great remake of

00:08:44.529 --> 00:08:48.779
The Monster Squad, particularly if it added
a fresh, modern take while parodying current

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:49.280
trends.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.720
Though nothing could beat the original, there's
definitely potential in a story that sees

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:56.620
a bunch of desensitized kids rolling their
eyes at the threat of a bunch of old-school

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:57.660
classic monsters.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:01.900
Maybe a Monster Squad remake could finally
bring that Blumhouse shared universe to fruition,

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:06.310
with a gang of kids sneeringly taking on the
horror studio's most notorious foes.

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:09.180
The fact that we haven't gotten a remake already
is completely:

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:11.700
"Bogus."

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:21.140
Starring Vincent Prince as a sour old thespian
who enacts ghastly acts of vengeance against

00:09:21.149 --> 00:09:25.760
his worst critics, this British tongue-in-cheek
horror outing is an underrated classic.

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.500
The film leans confidently into camp sensibilities
and takes great glee in some of the most preposterous,

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.509
and elaborate, murder scenes in horror comedy
history.

00:09:33.509 --> 00:09:37.970
The inspirations for each are taken from classic
deaths of various Shakespeare plays, which

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:41.500
played perfectly to early '70s British audiences
when the film was released.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:45.660
Although Theater of Blood continues to enjoy
a strong cult following, it's difficult to

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:48.780
imagine young audiences connecting with it
in quite the same way.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.240
However, in focusing on how critics can wield
their power, the underlying themes of Theater

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:54.420
of Blood remain familiar.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.660
After all, we live in a time when anyone can
take to social media to be as careless and

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:01.380
cruel as they like about art and the people
who make it.

